:: R3_TheDrawer [r3 story intimate]
<<location "Victoria Cross's Office, Floor 42">>
<<objective "Decide how to respond.">>

Inside the drawer, laid on dark velvet the color of a bruise, is a pair of black lace panties.

<<moodimage "files/images/items/la_perla_panties.png" "">>

I stare at them. My brain performs several operations simultaneously, like a computer running too many programs. The first operation is identification: La Perla. I know because Lena pointed them out in a magazine once, laughing, saying nobody actually buys those. Two hundred dollars for dental floss. The second operation is aesthetic recognition: they are beautiful. Black lace over a silk lining, delicate scalloped edge, a small satin bow at the front. They look like something a woman wears when she wants to feel expensive. The third operation is contextual processing: my boss is showing me women's underwear in her office at 7 AM while my career bleeds out on a conference room floor three stories below.

The fourth operation is the one I try to shut down. It doesn't shut down.

<<say "Victoria Cross">>Wear these tomorrow. Under your suit. All day. That's all I'm asking.<</say>>

She pauses. Her eyes are on my face with the focus of a jeweler examining a stone for flaws. Or for value.

<<say "Victoria Cross">>For now.<</say>>

For now. Two words that contain an entire future. A hallway with doors I can't see yet. A staircase going somewhere I haven't agreed to visit.

She picks the panties up from the velvet. Holds them out. Not to me. Between us. In the air between her hand and mine. The lace catches the morning light coming through the glass wall. The city glitters behind her. Forty-two stories of earned altitude.

<<say "Victoria Cross">>Hold them.<</say>>

I take them. The lace is lighter than I expected. Barely there. The silk lining is cool against my palm, slippery, the kind of fabric that doesn't want to stay still. My fingers close around them and the silk warms immediately, taking on my body heat like it was waiting for it.

<<modstat "feminization" 2>>

<<say "Victoria Cross">>Feel the fabric.<</say>>

She's watching my face. Cataloguing. I understand now what she's looking for. She's looking for three things: shame, confusion, and beneath both, something smaller. A spark. A catch in the breath that has nothing to do with horror and everything to do with curiosity.

I rub the silk between my thumb and forefinger. The lace edge has a texture like tiny raised letters in a language I don't know. The fabric is impossibly soft. Softer than anything in my drawer at home. Softer than anything I've ever worn against my skin. Lena's underwear is cotton, practical, bought in packs of six from Target. These are not that. These are crafted. Considered. They exist to make the wearer feel something specific.

Victoria stands and moves around the desk. She comes to stand behind my chair and slightly to my left. Close. Close enough that the perfume is no longer a suggestion. It's a statement. Dark and layered, something with oud and jasmine and smoke, something designed to fill the space between two people and make them aware of exactly how small that space is.

Her heels click on the hardwood. One. Two. They stop.

She's inches from me. I can feel her warmth the way you feel a radiator when you walk past it. Not touching. Present.

<<say "Victoria Cross">>Say 'yes, Ms. Cross.'<</say>>

My mouth is dry. My career is a flatline on a screen and she's holding the paddles. The panties are warm from my grip. My fingers have tightened around them without my permission, bunching the silk, pressing the lace into my palm. My palm is sweating and I wonder if she can tell. I wonder if that's part of what she's cataloguing.

I think about Greg Tanaka's face. The termination paperwork. The email I'd have to send to my father explaining why I lost another thing. The look on Lena's face when I tell her the career she married is gone. The apartment we can't afford on one salary. The plan I described over pad thai, dead at thirty-one, buried under a missed deadline and a misread room.

I think about all of that and underneath all of that, in the part of my brain I keep locked and unfurnished, I think about how the silk feels against my skin.

