:: R3_Choice_Negotiate [r3 story]
<<modstat "submission" 6>>
<<modstat "victoriaTrust" 1>>
<<npc "victoria" "trust" 1>>
<<objective "Get out of the office.">>

<<sayself>>Isn't there something else,<</sayself>>

I start.

Victoria raises one eyebrow. A single architectural motion. The eyebrow says everything her mouth doesn't bother with. It says: this is not a negotiation. It says: you walked into my office on Floor 42 with a dead career and sat in the shorter chair and I poured you coffee you didn't refuse. The negotiation happened before you opened my door. The terms were set before you pressed the elevator button. What's happening now is the part where you accept.

<<say "Victoria Cross">>Something else.<</say>>

Flat. Not a question.

<<sayself>>A different arrangement. I could work weekends. Take on additional accounts. There are other ways to demonstrate com-<</sayself>>

<<say "Victoria Cross">>Daniel.<</say>>

She says my name the way a teacher says it when the answer is wrong but the student is trying. Gentle. Final.

<<say "Victoria Cross">>This is what I'm offering. This, specifically. Not overtime. Not additional accounts. This.<</say>>

She holds the panties up. The lace sways slightly. The silk catches light.

I leave her office without agreeing. I ride the elevator down in silence. I sit at my desk for forty-five minutes staring at spreadsheets without reading a single number.

At 11 AM I send an email. One line.

//Yes, Ms. Cross.//

Her reply arrives in under thirty seconds.

//Good boy. I knew you'd get there.//

The response is worse than if she'd made me say it to her face. Because she wasn't even waiting. She was just doing her work, knowing I'd come around, the way you know the mail will arrive or the tide will come in. Inevitability as a management style.

I spend the rest of the day with the panties in my desk drawer, the bottom one, behind the hanging files where no one will look.

